  want one atomic thing to version and migrate.

  [1mB. One localStorage key per preference[0m
  [38;5;153mwebapp.pref.rememberedUsername[39m and so on, one key each. Writes touch only their own key, so concurrent tabs writing
  different preferences never conflict, and the [38;5;153mstorage[39m event tells you precisely which key changed.
  [3mTrades:[0m no single place to version, so migrating the set later means enumerating keys and a per-key migration; there's
  no atomic "all preferences" read or clear; and stray keys from removed features linger in storage invisibly. [3mWins [0m
  [3mwhen[0m preferences are numerous, written independently and frequently, or large enough that reserializing everything
  matters.

  [1mC. Blob plus a declared schema registry[0m
  Approach A, but each preference is declared up front with a default and a validator, and reads drop values failing
  validation. Guarantees callers never see a malformed value even if something else wrote to the key.
  [3mTrades:[0m a declaration layer and validator per preference — real ceremony for a module that starts with one string
  preference. [3mWins when[0m preferences are set by multiple writers, come from untrusted input, or feed code that breaks
  badly on a bad type.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. The preference set here is one string with an obvious default, and A's weakness — whole-blob writes and
  cross-tab clobbering — only bites at a scale this app is nowhere near, while its strength, one versioned object to
  migrate, is exactly what you want before the schema has been decided by accident. B's per-key independence buys
  nothing today and makes versioning harder later. C's validation is worth adding [3minside[0m A the day a preference has a
  type that can break a caller; paying for it now is speculative. A also doesn't foreclose the others: the
  [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m/[38;5;153msubscribe[39m interface is identical in all three, so the storage layout can change behind it later.
